last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2023-12-20T02:53:39.471465"}}},"ISBN:9781557784094":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9781557784094","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL1557811M/In_the_path_of_the_masters","preview":"restricted","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/inpathofmastersu0000carm","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/781072-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Paragon House"],"identifiers":{"librarything":["1205664"],"goodreads":["1673287"]},"subtitle":"understanding the spirituality of Buddha, Confucius, Jesus, and Muhammad","covers":[781072],"local_id":["urn:mgc:31927000257102","urn:cst:10017011518"],"lc_classifications":["BL624 .C347 1994","BL624.C347 1994","BL624 .C347 1993"],"key":"/books/OL1557811M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL242214A","name":"Denise Lardner Carmody"}],"ocaid":"inpathofmastersu0000carm","publish_places":["New York"],"contributions":["Carmody, John, 1939-"],"subjects":["Spiritual life.","Religious biography.","Religions.","Religions -- Relations."],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"pagination":"232 p. ;","source_records":["amazon:1557784094","marc:marc_marygrove/marygrovecollegelibrary.full.D20191108.T213022.internetarchive2nd_REPACK.mrc:111820125:4720","ia:inpathofmastersu0000carm","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC1_barcode.mrc:192941832:4669","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part21.utf8:24201909:982","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC1_multibarcode.mrc:193198883:4669","bwb:9781557784094","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-003.mrc:456888442:3677","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.02.20150123.full.mrc:512354127:848","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_032.bib.mrc:262934791:1601","marc:UniversityOfMichiganMarcCatalogRecords/umich_bib.mrc:288420905:842"],"title":"In the path of the masters","dewey_decimal_class":["291.6/3"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references and index."},"number_of_pages":232,"edition_name":"1st ed.","lccn":["91038574"],"isbn_10":["1557784094"],"publish_date":"1994","publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"Denise Lardner Carmody and John Tully Carmody.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL2010820W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"oclc_numbers":["25131391","30841924"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"How should we live? What is the path to peace, wisdom, and fulfillment? Certainly, these are central questions in our lives. Who better to consult for guidance than Buddha, Confucius, Jesus, and Muhammad? By reflecting on the lives of these great teachers, In the Path of the Masters illuminates the practical and personal implications of prayer and devotion and shows how spirituality can help us lead meaningful lives.\n\nIn this beautifully written book the Carmodys offer readers a gift: the chance to take another look at and learn from figures who may be so familiar we don't - or can't - see them any more. They offer a brief biography of each founder, describing the events that most shaped his life, how his personal spirituality developed, how he lived, and how he died. Without resorting to useless speculation, they also describe as much as they can what kind of person he was.\n\nWhat made him angry; what made him laugh, who his enemies and friends were. Finally, they briefly trace the course of each religious tradition after its founder's death.\n\n.\n\nMost helpful of all, In the Path of the Masters cuts through dogma and interpretation and goes to the heart of the matter: the lessons that each founder considered most important for a fulfilling life. Divided into the major dimensions of spiritual life - nature, society, the self, and divinity - the Carmodys provide clear and easy access to where each figure stands on enduring issues and how they compare with each other.\n\nThe Buddha, for example, did not believe in a solid, substantial self although he did hand down a basic ethical code to his followers. Confucius focused on the Way, or traditional wisdom, as the guiding force behind personal development; Jesus looked to God, his father, as a way of understanding himself; Muhammad believed that the first relationship the self had to establish correctly was with Allah.\n\n. As we face the difficulties of the twenty-first century, regardless of religious upbringing, the Carmodys show what the spiritual lives and lessons of these masters offer us. Evenhanded in approach, but passionate in the conviction that spirituality enriches modern life, In the Path of the Masters is a thought-provoking and enlightening guide to inner fulfillment."},"latest_revision":16,"revision":16,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-05-15T04:40:18.860748"}}},"ISBN:9781281162410":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9781281162410","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL40418539M/Ritual_and_Its_Consequences","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL40418539M/Ritual_and_Its_Consequences","details":{"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL2769636A","name":"Adam B. Seligman"},{"key":"/authors/OL4764704A","name":"Robert P. Weller"},{"key":"/authors/OL10854508A","name":"Robert P. Simon"}],"isbn_13":["9781281162410"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"pagination":"229","publish_date":"2008","publishers":["Oxford University Press"],"source_records":["bwb:9781281162410"],"title":"Ritual and Its Consequences","subtitle":"An Essay on the Limits of Sincerity","full_title":"Ritual and Its Consequences An Essay on the Limits of Sincerity","works":[{"key":"/works/OL19818635W"}],"key":"/books/OL40418539M","latest_revision":1,"revision":1,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2022-11-18T16:04:33.843395"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2022-11-18T16:04:33.843395"}}},"ISBN:9780195336016":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9780195336016","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL10135335M/Ritual_and_its_Consequences","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL10135335M/Ritual_and_its_Consequences","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/2330258-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Oxford University Press, USA","Oxford University Press"],"number_of_pages":248,"subtitle":"An Essay on the Limits of Sincerity","covers":[2330258],"local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223088625851"],"physical_format":"Paperback","lc_classifications":["BL600.R5775 2008","BL600 .R5775 2008"],"key":"/books/OL10135335M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL2769636A","name":"Adam B. Seligman"},{"key":"/authors/OL715089A","name":"Robert P. Weller"},{"key":"/authors/OL3376327A","name":"Michael J Puett"},{"key":"/authors/OL1120954A","name":"Bennett Simon"}],"subjects":["Religion & Beliefs","Religious rites & ceremonies","Religion / Comparative Religion","Anthropology - Cultural","Comparative Religion","Sociology of Religion","Religion","Religion - World Religions"],"isbn_13":["9780195336016"],"source_records":["marc:marc_loc_updates/v36.i16.records.utf8:6820803:1118","marc:marc_loc_updates/v36.i50.records.utf8:4979180:1131","marc:marc_loc_updates/v36.i25.records.utf8:10311422:1131","marc:marc_loc_updates/v37.i35.records.utf8:15356428:1226","marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run04.mrc:44101884:2346","bwb:9780195336016","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part34.utf8:134048380:1226","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-014.mrc:7548047:1450","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.11.20150123.full.mrc:377555249:2496","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_016.bib.mrc:73142181:3725"],"title":"Ritual and its Consequences","identifiers":{"goodreads":["3061109"],"librarything":["5271375"]},"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"isbn_10":["0195336011"],"publish_date":"February 22, 2008","works":[{"key":"/works/OL18631192W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"physical_dimensions":"0.1 x 0.1 x 0.1 inches","lccn":["2007026538"],"oclc_numbers":["153773006"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"\"This pioneering, interdisciplinary work shows how rituals allow us to live in a perennially imperfect world. Drawing on a variety of cultural settings, the authors utilize psychoanalytic and anthropological perspectives to describe how ritual - like play - creates \"as if\" worlds, rooted in the imaginative capacity of the human mind to create a subjunctive universe. The ability to cross between imagined worlds is central to the human capacity for empathy. Ritual, they claim, defines the boundaries of these imagined worlds, including those of empathy and other realms of human creativity, such as music, architecture and literature.\" \"The authors juxtapose this ritual orientation to a \"sincere\" search for unity and wholeness. The sincere world sees fragmentation and incoherence as signs of inauthenticity that must be overcome. Our modern world has accepted the sincere viewpoint at the expense of ritual, dismissing ritual as mere convention. In response, the authors show how the conventions of ritual allow us to live together in a broken world. Ritual is work, endless work. But it is among the most important things that we humans do.\"--Jacket."},"latest_revision":18,"revision":18,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-30T09:38:13.731961"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-08-28T18:44:49.043209"}}},"ISBN:9780195336009":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9780195336009","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL10135334M/Ritual_and_Its_Consequences","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL10135334M/Ritual_and_Its_Consequences","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/5424040-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Oxford University Press, USA","Oxford University Press"],"number_of_pages":248,"subtitle":"An Essay on the Limits of Sincerity","covers":[5424040,5037050],"physical_format":"Hardcover","lc_classifications":["BL600.R5775 2008"],"key":"/books/OL10135334M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL27